\fs96 \cf2 Materializing compile-time values at run time\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo's compile-time metaprogramming makes it easy to make calculations at compile time for later use. The process of making a compile-time value available at run time is called materialization. For types that can be trivially copied, this isn't an issue. The compiler can simply insert the value into the compiled program wherever it's needed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 comptime\cf13  threshold\cf5 :\cf13  Int \cf5 =\cf13  some_calculation\cf5 ()\cf13   \cf6 # calculate at compile time\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf15 1000\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     my_function\cf5 (\cf13 i\cf5 ,\cf13  threshold\cf5 )\cf13   \cf6 # use value at runtime\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However, Mojo also allows you to create instances of much more complex types at compile-time: types that dynamically allocate memory, like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Re-using these values at run time presents some questions, like where the memory is allocated, who owns the values, and when the values are destroyed.\
This page describes when Mojo materializes values, and presents some techniques for avoiding unnecessary materialization of complex values.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Implicit and explicit materialization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When you use a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value at run time, you're explicitly or implicitly copying the value into a run-time variable:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 comptime\cf13  comptime_value \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 1000\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb12 var\cf13  runtime_value \cf5 =\cf13  comptime_value\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This process of moving a compile-time value to a run-time variable is called materialization. If the value is implicitly copyable, like an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Bool
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , Mojo treats it as implicitly materializable as well.\
But types that aren't implicitly copyable present other challenges. Consider the following code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 lookup_fn\cf5 (\cf13 count\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 comptime\cf13  list_of_values \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 5\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 7\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     \cf5 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf13 count\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         \cf6 # Some computation, doesn't matter what it is.\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         idx \cf5 =\cf13  dynamic_function\cf5 (\cf13 i\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12         \cf6 # Look up another value\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         lookup \cf5 =\cf13  list_of_values\cf5 [\cf13 idx\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12         \cf6 # Use the value\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         process\cf5 (\cf13 lookup\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This looks reasonable, but compiling it produces an error on this line:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 lookup \cf5 =\cf13  list_of_values\cf5 [\cf13 idx\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 cannot materialize comptime value of type 'List[Int]' to runtime\cb1 \
\cb12 because it is not 'ImplicitlyCopyable'\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Just like Mojo forces you to explicitly copy a value that's expensive to copy, it forces you to explicitly materialize values that are expensive to materialize, by calling the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/value/materialize/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 materialize()}} function.\
Here's the code above with explicit materialization added:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 lookup_fn\cf5 (\cf13 count\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 comptime\cf13  list_of_values \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 5\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 7\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     \cf5 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf13 count\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         idx \cf5 =\cf13  dynamic_function\cf5 (\cf13 i\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12         \cf6 # This is the problem\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         \cf5 var\cf13  tmp\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  materialize\cf5 [\cf13 list_of_values\cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         lookup \cf5 =\cf13  tmp\cf5 [\cf13 idx\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         \cf6 # tmp is destroyed here\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12         process\cf5 (\cf13 lookup\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This code materializes the list of values inside of the loop, which includes dynamically allocating heap memory and storing the four elements into that memory. Because the last use of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 tmp
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is on the next line, the memory then gets deallocated before the loop iterates. This creates and destroys the list on every iteration of the loop, which is clearly wasteful.\
A more efficient version would materialize the list outside of the loop:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 lookup_fn\cf5 (\cf13 count\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 comptime\cf13  list_of_values \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 5\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 7\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  \cf14 list\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  materialize\cf5 [\cf13 list_of_values\cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf14 range\cf5 (\cf13 count\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         idx \cf5 =\cf13  dynamic_function\cf5 (\cf13 i\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12         lookup \cf5 =\cf13  \cf14 list\cf5 [\cf13 idx\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12         process\cf5 (\cf13 lookup\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf6 # materialized list is destroyed here\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This is why Mojo requires you to explicitly materialize non-trivial values; it puts you in control of when your program allocates resources.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Global lookup tables\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo doesn't currently have a general-purpose mechanism for creating global static data. This is a problem for some performance-sensitive code where you want to use a static lookup table. Even if you declare the table as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value, you need to materialize it each time you want to use the data.\
The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/globals/global_constant/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 global_constant()}} function provides a solution for storing a compile-time value into static global storage, so you can access it without repeatedly materializing the value. However, this currently only works for self-contained values which don't include pointers to other locations in memory. That rules out using collection types like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
The easiest way to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 global_constant()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/inline_array/InlineArray/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 InlineArray}}, which allocates a statically sized array of elements on the stack. The following code uses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 global_constant()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to create a static lookup table.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 builtin\cf5 .\cf14 globals\cf13  \cf5 import\cf13  global_constant\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 use_lookup\cf5 (\cf13 idx\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 )\cf13  \cf5 ->\cf13  Int64\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 comptime\cf13  numbers\cf5 :\cf13  InlineArray\cf5 [\cf13 Int64\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 10\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12         \cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 14\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 34\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 63\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 101\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 148\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 204\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 269\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 343\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 ref\cf13  lookup_table \cf5 =\cf13  global_constant\cf5 [\cf13 numbers\cf5 ]()\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 if\cf13  idx \cf5 >=\cf13  \cf14 len\cf5 (\cf13 lookup_table\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12           \cf5 return\cf13  \cf15 0\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 return\cf13  lookup_table\cf5 [\cf13 idx\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf13 use_lookup\cf5 (\cf15 3\cf5 ))\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 At compile time, Mojo allocates the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 numbers
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  array, and then the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 global_constant()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method copies it into static constant memory, where the code can reference it without requiring any dynamic logic to create or populate the array. At run time, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 lookup_table
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  identifier receives an immutable reference to this memory.\
Note the use of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 ref lookup_table
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to bind the reference returned by 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 global_constant()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 var lookup_table
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  would cause a compiler error, because it would trigger a copy, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 InlineArray
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  doesn't support implicit copying.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Using the 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  keyword\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Another approach that you can use to avoid materializing a complex value is to use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword to control when Mojo evaluates an expression. Assigning an expression to a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value causes Mojo to evaluate the expression at compile time.\
For example, if you want to force a function to run at compile time:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 comptime\cf13  tmp \cf5 =\cf13  calculate_something\cf5 ()\cf13   \cf6 # executed at compile time\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb12 var\cf13  y \cf5 =\cf13  x \cf5 *\cf13  tmp  \cf6 # executed at run time\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If you're only creating a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value for a single use, you can use a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  sub-expression instead:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 var\cf13  y \cf5 =\cf13  x \cf5 *\cf13  \cf5 comptime\cf13  \cf5 (\cf13 calculate_something\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This works exactly like the previous example, without creating a named temporary value. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword here tells Mojo to evaluate the expression inside the parentheses (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 calculate_something()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) at compile time.\
For example, you can use a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  sub-expression when working with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Layout
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type, which determines how you store and retrieve data in a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Materializing a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Layout
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  requires dynamic allocation, which isn't supported on GPUs. So calling this code on a GPU produces an error:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 comptime\cf13  layout \cf5 =\cf13  Layout\cf5 .\cf13 row_major\cf5 (\cf15 16\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 8\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb12 var\cf13  x \cf5 =\cf13  layout\cf5 .\cf13 size\cf5 ()\cf13  \cf5 //\cf13  WARP_SIZE  \cf6 # Can't implicitly materialize layout\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  sub-expression fixes this issue:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 comptime\cf13  layout \cf5 =\cf13  Layout\cf5 .\cf13 row_major\cf5 (\cf15 16\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 8\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb12 var\cf13  x \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 comptime\cf13  \cf5 (\cf13 layout\cf5 .\cf13 size\cf5 ())\cf13  \cf5 //\cf13  WARP_SIZE\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Now, the expression 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 layout.size()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  gets evaluated at compile time, so there's no need to materialize the layout.\
You could also achieve the same effect using a named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value.\
